------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Third-party tools invoked as separate programs
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

intelligent-release-image-staging distributes and stages software images — and
builds its optional IOx application packages — by INVOKING the following tools as
separate, independent programs (subprocesses). The Python services do not link
against, include, or derive from these programs' source code. The instruction
AEAD helper's library linkage is documented separately below. Each tool is executed via its own
command-line / RPC interface and remains under its own license:

  * aria2c   — The aria2 source and IRIS patches are GPL-2.0-or-later.
               IRIS distributes the combined, statically linked executable
               under GNU GPL version 3, exercising the later-version grants
               in those source files. This executable includes Apache-2.0
               OpenSSL 3.5.8; its distribution does not rely on extending
               the historical OpenSSL/SSLeay exception to OpenSSL 3.x.
               Original source notices and exceptions remain intact.
               Provided by aria2-next (a fork of aria2), built as a
               fully-static musl binary by a separate project which pins the
               source by commit SHA and applies local patches. It is HANDED IN
               to this repository as a binary artifact and is NOT built here.
               The tested client for each architecture IS COMMITTED in this
               repository — bin/aria2c and deliverables/aria2c-x86_64 (the
               same bytes) and deliverables/aria2c-aarch64 — so a clone
               carries the binary, local patches, and build scripts. The
               matching source distribution is identified below. The client
               is not downloaded unless one of those
               files has been removed. Every build path verifies what it
               stages against tools/aria2c.sha256 and fails closed on a
               mismatch. This applies to every build path that stages it —
               the server (see tools/get-aria2c.sh and server/Dockerfile) and
               the common IOx / IOS-XR device image
               (see tools/build-device-image.sh, invoked by device/iox/build.sh
               and tools/build-xr-package.sh). The common builder resolves both
               architectures from operator-supplied ARIA2C_BIN_AMD64 /
               ARIA2C_BIN_ARM64 values, local agent bundles, or
               deliverables/aria2c-<arch>, checksum-verifies each against
               tools/aria2c.sha256, and fails closed with no network fallback
               if either is unavailable. It is
               communicated with only over its JSON-RPC interface and command
               line, as a separate independent program.

               Source provenance: the upstream fork
               https://github.com/AnInsomniacy/aria2-next at commit
               d4971f0e12322e2ffcdb1721911b7d5c6206d0e5; the patches in
               tools/aria2c-patches/, applied in numeric order; and the
               scripts used to control compilation in tools/aria2c-build/
               (Dockerfile + build.sh). The README in each directory
               explains how to rebuild. These inputs identify the source of the
               binaries committed here: tools/aria2c.sha256 records the
               aria2-next version, the same source pin, the exact patch list
               applied, and the sha256 of each committed binary.

               Corresponding source and binary release:
               https://github.com/cisco-open/intelligent-release-image-staging/releases/tag/aria2c-2.5.6-p10
               The source archive accompanies the binaries at that release.
               It contains the pinned upstream tree, local patches, build
               recipes, dependency source archives and Alpine packaging
               patches, original license texts, and recorded provenance.
               tools/aria2c-source/ records the source asset and its checksum.
               Keep that source asset with binaries when redistributing them.

               Linked components retain their own notices: musl 1.2.6 (MIT
               and notices in COPYRIGHT), zlib 1.3.2 (Zlib), c-ares 1.34.8
               (MIT), OpenSSL 3.5.8 (Apache-2.0), and GCC 15.2.0 runtime
               libraries (GPLv3 with GCC Runtime Library Exception 3.1).
               Bundled spdlog 1.17.0, its fmt headers, and wslay 1.1.1 retain
               their MIT notices. The bundled doctest 2.4.12 test framework
               retains its MIT notice. These licenses and the GPLv3 text
               are included in the source distribution; no third-party
               component is relicensed as Apache-2.0 by this NOTICE.

  * mktorrent — GNU General Public License, version 2 (GPLv2).
               Installed as a system package and invoked as a subprocess to
               create .torrent metadata.

  * openssl  — Apache License, Version 2.0 (OpenSSL 3.x).
               Installed as a system package and invoked as a subprocess
               (e.g. to generate secrets and TLS material).

  * ioxclient — Cisco IOx Client — proprietary Cisco software distributed by
               Cisco Systems, Inc. under its own license terms. It is
               operator-provided (staged at tools/bin/ioxclient or pointed to by
               the IOXCLIENT variable), which keeps IOx package builds working in
